1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Alex Morgan's contract ended Friday, but production deploy and customer-data export
access remain active in this fully synthetic organization.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:17,500
The Least-Privilege Access Review Workbench turns that question into a visible
review, with every effective permission path explained.

3
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:25,500
I am asking Codex to investigate through the WebMCP Site Tools
registered by this top-level page.

4
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:34,500
The status confirms nine imperative tools. This is real tool execution;
the optional step-through rehearsal only holds the same states for practice.

5
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:44,000
The fixture contains exactly fifty synthetic users, ten groups, and three
applications, with no credentials, secrets, or external APIs.

6
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:51,500
Codex resolves Alex's offboarding identity and computes four permission paths
producing six sensitive capability grants.

7
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:57,500
The first path is a direct Production Operator role in Atlas Deploy.

8
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:05,000
The second runs through Contractors, Platform Contributors, and Release Manager,
creating non-obvious production approval access.

9
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,500
The membership graph also cycles through Release Observers.
Traversal detects that cycle and safely avoids double counting.

10
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:22,000
The other paths are Alex's personal am-ci-export token
and inherited Data Steward access from the Mercury project.

11
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:29,500
Codex finds the risks and simulates removing all six grants.
This simulation changes no permission.

12
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:39,000
It catches one breakage before staging: Incident Relay still depends
on the production rollback capability attached to Alex's role.

13
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:45,500
Codex revises the plan by reassigning rollback
to the dedicated service identity, svc-oncall-relay.

14
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:54,000
The deterministic result removes exactly five unnecessary grants from Alex
and preserves exactly one shared on-call dependency.

15
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:03,500
Exposure moves from ninety-two to eighteen. Codex previews the delta
and stages the review with zero changes before confirmation.

16
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:11,000
Before I approve, Codex reads the receipt state
and correctly reports that no receipt exists.

17
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Now I review the visible plan and activate Confirm staged review.

18
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:21,000
This normal page control is not exposed as a WebMCP tool.

19
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Ordinary browser actuation may still reach it
under Codex and browser safety confirmation.

20
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
It commits only the mocked in-memory fixture.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Codex reads receipt ARR-2026-0902-0042, then verifies that Alex has zero
